    "source": "/** Strictly increasing LIS in O(n log n) with recovery. Returns [length, one longest strictly increasing subsequence...]. */\npublic class Lis {\n    static long[] lis(long[] a) {\n        int n = a.length;\n        int[] tails = new int[n]; // tails[k] = index of the smallest possible tail of length k+1\n        int len = 0;\n        int[] prev = new int[n];\n        java.util.Arrays.fill(prev, -1);\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            int lo = 0, hi = len;\n            while (lo < hi) {\n                int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;\n                if (a[tails[mid]] < a[i]) lo = mid + 1;\n                else hi = mid;\n            }\n            if (lo > 0) prev[i] = tails[lo - 1];\n            tails[lo] = i;\n            if (lo == len) len++;\n        }\n        long[] res = new long[len + 1];\n        res[0] = len;\n        int m = len;\n        for (int k = len > 0 ? tails[len - 1] : -1; k >= 0; k = prev[k]) res[m--] = a[k];\n        return res;\n    }\n}\n",
